ced pi3 kinase akt mtor signaling  the hallmarks of  the ti3 kinase signaling pathway  were  less in pd-1 deficiency rigs higher in  wild-type t-regs  similarly the phospho-akt  as shown on the right  in blue in wild-type t-cells you've got  phospho-akt but in the pd-1 deficient  t-regs there was less fossil akt  now it's known and has been published by  krellen and others that increase pi3  pathway activity results in reduced  t-reg suppressive capacity  and we wondered uh whether this  pathway was what was resulting in the  increased t-reg function  now pi3-kinase akt signaling regulates  glycolysis and mitochondrial functions  and here  it's shown that  pd1 deficient t-regs are less glycolytic  here in red you can see they have a  lower  extracellular acidification rate in a  seahorse assay  and the glycolytic capacity  is less in the td1 deficient t-regs and  they have less glycolytic reserve  similarly  the pd-1 deficient t-regs have an  increased  mitochondrial function you can see uh  here in red  they have an increased oxygen  consumption rate  increased spare respiratory capacity and  increased maximal  oxygen consumption rate  consistent with increased mitochondrial  functions  now  if this is the case um increasing phosph  akt activity should reverse this  and  here in the middle you show the  condition without an akt agonist and  indeed the pd-1 deficient  um t-regs are less suppressive  more suppressive  but  um if  they're treated with an akt agonist  the amount of suppressive capacity of  wild-type or pd1 deficient t-regs is  lost and both  suppress equally efficiently  so in summary of this section what i say  is that pd1 restrains both t effector  activity as is well known  but  to our  somewhat surprised  cd1 also restrains t-reg activities  so pd-1 on t-regs limits their  activation and suppressive capacity and  if you lose or block pd-1 you get  better suppressive activity  now this coordinates very well with some  recent papers from the nishikaw